WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.823
- This is not good for my body,

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.093
but it's so good for my everything else.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.510
(upbeat, string music)

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.120
- Today, us two Americans are going to try

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.430
afternoon tea for the first time

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.610
in the heart of London.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.670
- Although this guy is in it,

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.650
does not mean that it's a Worth It video,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.630
I'm not Andrew.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.463
- You're not Andrew?

00:00:19.463 --> 00:00:20.420
- No, I'm not.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.242
- Oh my gosh, I couldn't tell!

00:00:22.242 --> 00:00:23.900
- Are you ready to have a tea party?

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:24.733
- Cheers.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:25.863
(ding sound)

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:26.696
- Thank you for joining us here.

00:00:26.696 --> 00:00:28.780
- Traditionally, afternoon tea comprise of

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.730
an afternoon tea stand.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.290
Afternoon tea is basically

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.693
to bridge the gap
between lunch and dinner,

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.580
and it comes with three tiers.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.333
On the bottom you'd have
your savory sandwich tier,

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.070
traditionally finger sandwiches.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.220
And then moving up,
you'd have a scone tier,

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.670
with scones, traditionally
clotted cream and jam.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:46.503
And then, on the top,

00:00:46.503 --> 00:00:48.390
you'd finish with all of
your delicate pastries.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.580
And that'd also be
accompanied with a cup of tea.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.010
Today, I'm going to be preparing

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.327
our Charbonnel-inspired afternoon tea.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:57.482
- So, tea is just one
component of afternoon tea.

00:00:57.482 --> 00:00:58.970
Did the focus of afternoon tea--

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.560
- Traditionally, it's the whole package.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.180
- What is the right way to go
about this whole experience?

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.013
- And the pinkie.

00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:05.350
- Yeah, does the pinkie go out?

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:06.553
- No pinkie.

00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:07.683
- No pinkie?
- No pinkie?

00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:08.516
- Is that just pretentious?

00:01:08.516 --> 00:01:09.360
(laughs)

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.351
- I don't know where the pinkie came from.

00:01:11.351 --> 00:01:13.650
(upbeat music)

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.100
So you take your filter,
and then you pour it in.

00:01:16.462 --> 00:01:17.330
- What is the filter for?

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.544
- It catches the tea leaves,

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.540
because traditionally,
you wouldn't use a tea bag

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.503
with your tea, it's loose leaves.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.620
- Does that make the flavor
even broader in the tea?

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.453
- Yeah.

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:28.200
- Do people cheers when they do their tea?

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.793
- I've never cheersed to a tea, but--

00:01:29.793 --> 00:01:30.626
- You've never cheersed?

00:01:30.626 --> 00:01:31.861
Well, you're about to right now.

00:01:31.861 --> 00:01:32.694
(laughs)

00:01:32.694 --> 00:01:33.793
- You don't know us so, cheers!

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:34.626
- Cheers!

00:01:34.643 --> 00:01:35.790
- We cheers everything.

00:01:37.777 --> 00:01:40.360
(upbeat music)

00:01:40.453 --> 00:01:41.570
- Ooh, beautiful!

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:42.680
- That looks amazing!

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.533
- For some reason it
reminds me of a dollhouse,

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.820
it's so properly put together.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.970
I don't even want to eat it.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.520
- So, as tempting as it
may be to start at the top,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.070
unfortunately you have
to start at the bottom,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.220
and then you work your
way up through the tiers,

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.123
from savory, to sweet and savory,

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:58.533
to finish on the sweet.

00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:01.110
(upbeat music)

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.820
- We have a selection of five
different finger sandwiches:

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.030
smoked salmon is quite popular,

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.490
cucumber and cream cheese
is ever so popular as well,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.600
we've done a twist on the
(mumbles) tomato focaccia,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.060
we have a ham and mustard there,

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.610
a red onion and cream cheese,

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.450
and then at the back we
have beef and horseradish.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.490
- So finger sandwiches.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.930
At first I was worried that
these would be sandwiches

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:21.610
made out of fingers of
different animals, but it's not.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.423
- Yeah, I was worried about that too.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.333
Whoo.
- I mean

00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:26.000
you're telling me that thought
didn't pop into your head

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.200
for a second when he
said finger sandwiches?

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:29.843
All right.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.083
- Its' so easy to eat, it's almost scary.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.680
- I feel like I could eat
20 of these, like popcorn.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.100
- I have the roast beef here.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.843
Roast beef, it's like a
really rich mayonnaise.

00:02:42.456 --> 00:02:43.743
- That's really good.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.790
- This is cool, cause these
are the kind of sandwiches

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.750
that I would make at home.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.020
- They're very simple,
there's something like,

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.220
nostalgic about it, like this is something

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.663
my mom would make me,
like going off to school.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.040
- I like this experience,
because this makes me feel

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.003
a little posh.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.430
- A little pampered.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.010
- Somebody is putting
it in the easiest form

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.070
for you to just put it in your mouth.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:03.863
All right.

00:03:04.204 --> 00:03:05.603
Round one
- Tier one complete.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.000
- I'm already pretty full.

00:03:07.275 --> 00:03:09.570
(upbeat music)

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.880
- The middle tier is two types of scones,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.730
we have a Sicilian lemon
scone, and we have a

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.683
Charbonnel et Walker chocolate scone.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.200
Traditionally it's served
with clotted cream and jam,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.440
but we've done our little spin on it,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.790
and we've done vanilla mascarpone,
and an orange marmalade.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.050
You break the scone, and then
it's up to you whether you

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.950
cream first, and then
jam, or jam and cream.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.020
- What do you, I know
there's a lot of controversy.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.267
- For me, you've got to
put the cream on first.

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:35.780
- I'm going for the lemon one first.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.120
- Yeah, cause they're
already pre-sliced almost.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.040
- Cream first.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.980
- Oh my god, that is thick.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.080
- Did you just eat without
me, without cheersing?

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.630
- You're making a sandwich?

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:44.893
- A proper scone.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:52.200
That's really good, it's not dry at all.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.220
It looks like it's crumbly,
like to the point of dry.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.600
But in your mouth, with the
cream cheese, and the jam,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.650
it really helps solidify the whole thing.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:01.700
It comes together.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.570
- Perfect combination of flavors.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.653
- Chop into this guy.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.450
- Oh man, I'm making a mess over here.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.283
- Yeah.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.133
- All right.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.050
- It looks like egg yolk.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:10.393
Cheers, old bloke.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:11.303
- Cheers.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.767
(ding sound)

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:14.903
It's so good.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:16.500
What a great tea party this is.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.060
- Oh my god, this is awesome.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.813
(upbeat music)

00:04:34.598 --> 00:04:36.793
- Look at that, it's a powdered donut,

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:38.490
and you dip it in chocolate.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:39.523
- Oh my god.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:40.573
- Dunk that.

00:04:40.573 --> 00:04:41.510
- This looks so good.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:43.480
- Look at that.

00:04:43.921 --> 00:04:44.754
- Cheers

00:04:44.754 --> 00:04:45.587
- Cheers.

00:04:48.174 --> 00:04:50.832
- We've reached the seventh
layer of hell, whatever it is.

00:04:50.832 --> 00:04:52.415
We are in pure sin.

00:04:53.439 --> 00:04:57.022
(techno dance party music)

00:04:59.572 --> 00:05:01.112
You know how I know it's so wrong?

00:05:01.112 --> 00:05:03.009
- Cause it tastes so good?

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:03.842
Cheers.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:10.813
Caramel.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.770
The crumpet, this must be the...

00:05:13.796 --> 00:05:14.629
- No.

00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:15.462
- No?

00:05:15.462 --> 00:05:16.295
- It's a brownie.

00:05:16.295 --> 00:05:17.128
- Oh, it's a brownie.

00:05:17.128 --> 00:05:18.110
- First pour the chocolate on top.

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.590
- Oh my god, go big or go home.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.453
- Let the rain flow.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.250
Let the sin blow over it.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.290
- Dude I always pictured
afternoon tea as this very light,

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:33.305
sophisticated, like,
we're going ham over here.

00:05:33.305 --> 00:05:34.138
- Oh god.

00:05:34.138 --> 00:05:36.660
- That's a unique flavor
right there, it's not just

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:37.580
straight-up chocolate.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:38.930
- There's like a lemon
or something in there.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:39.763
It's really good.

00:05:39.763 --> 00:05:40.963
Look. I'm dead.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:43.103
We did it, we had afternoon tea.

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:44.123
- We did.

00:05:44.123 --> 00:05:46.490
- I feel like we are in
the British version of

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:47.933
Sex in the City right now.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.530
I don't know what those
people do in that show,

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.630
but they're always hanging
out in cafe's at 2pm.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.683
- How people do this in
between lunch and dinner,

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.620
commonly, is beyond me.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.483
- I did enjoy it, it was a good time.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:00.383
- I did too.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.780
You know, it's exciting, you
get such a variety of things.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.130
You get to go on a little
adventure with your friend

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.080
while you hang out.

00:06:06.444 --> 00:06:08.944
(light music)

